<TopicPage slug="subjects/creative-writing/foundational/craft-image-detail-and-the-sentence">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Creative writing can be learned — that is the workshop's founding bet. The craft's bedrock is
    deceptively small: images instead of abstractions, specific details instead of general claims,
    sentences whose rhythm does work, and the discipline to revise. Everything genre-specific (plot
    architecture, metre, form) stands on these four habits.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bedrock habits of the craft" widget="equationStep" config={craftCfg} />

  <h2>Why images out-argue statements</h2>
  <p>
    Readers believe what they are shown and resist what they are told. "The village was poor"
    invites doubt; "the church clock was right twice a day only by accident" invites a picture, and
    the poverty arrives unargued. This is not decoration — it is rhetoric for fiction, the way
    writers make invented facts carry evidential weight.
  </p>

  <h2>The sentence as the writer's instrument</h2>
  <p>
    Style lives below the paragraph. Coordination vs subordination sets causality; word order sets
    emphasis (the end of the sentence is its loudest slot); length sets tempo. A useful exercise
    from the workshops: take one sentence of yours and rewrite it five ways — dialogue-snappy,
    accumulating, delayed-verb, passive, fragment — then notice what each version claims.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading as a writer</h2>
  <p>
    The workshop's other engine is逆向-engineering published work: not "what does this mean?" but
    "how did the writer do that?" Why does this paragraph start with dialogue? Where did the
    tension turn? Writers read with the clockwork exposed. Keep a commonplace book of moves, steal
    consciously, and the apprenticeship compounds — which is the honest secret of the writing life.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Iowa Writers' Workshop curriculum model; Burroway, <em>Writing Fiction</em>; Prose, <em>Reading Like a Writer</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
